sections.s2_technical:
HATEOAS is the highest level of REST maturity, the top of the Richardson Maturity Model. The idea is that the client should not need to know URLs in advance. It makes one entry request, and every response includes a set of links telling it what it can do next, so the application state is driven by the hypermedia the server returns. A transfer that is in a pending state might include a link to cancel it; once it is settled, that cancel link disappears and a link to view the receipt appears. The client reacts to the links present rather than hardcoding rules about which actions are valid.

In practice this is expressed with a link format. A common one is HAL, Hypertext Application Language, where a response has a `_links` object mapping relation names to hrefs.

```json
{
  "accountNumber": "SAV2001",
  "balance": 1000000,
  "_links": {
    "self":         { "href": "/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001" },
    "transactions": { "href": "/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/transactions" },
    "transfer":     { "href": "/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/transfers" }
  }
}
```

In Spring you build this with Spring HATEOAS. You wrap a DTO in an `EntityModel` and attach links built from controller methods, so the links stay correct even if routes change.

```java
EntityModel<AccountResponse> model = EntityModel.of(dto);
model.add(linkTo(methodOn(AccountController.class).get("SAV2001")).withSelfRel());
model.add(linkTo(methodOn(TransferController.class).create("SAV2001")).withRel("transfer"));
return model;
```

The promised benefits are real in theory. Clients become loosely coupled to URL structure, so the server can change its URLs without breaking clients that follow links. The server can convey valid state transitions dynamically, so a client never attempts an action that is not currently allowed. And the API becomes self-describing and discoverable from a single entry point.

Yet most real-world APIs skip full HATEOAS, for several reasons. The first is client complexity: writing a client that genuinely discovers behavior by parsing links at runtime is far harder than calling known URLs, and in practice almost all clients hardcode the URLs anyway, ignoring the links, so the server did extra work for nothing. The second is that the benefit it promises is largely delivered more cheaply by good documentation: OpenAPI and Swagger describe every endpoint, parameter, response, and status code in a machine-readable spec that generates client SDKs, interactive docs, and server stubs, which is what teams actually want. The third is payload bloat and server effort: every response carries link metadata, and the server must compute which transitions are valid, adding size and code for a feature clients rarely exploit.

A balanced, senior view is that HATEOAS is not all-or-nothing. Many APIs adopt a pragmatic middle ground: they include a few genuinely useful links, such as pagination links for next and previous pages, or a link to a related resource, without committing to full hypermedia-driven state. Pagination links are the one place HATEOAS ideas are widely adopted because they clearly help. But full HATEOAS, where the client is a generic hypermedia agent, is rare outside a few standards-driven domains. The practical default is a well-versioned, well-documented REST API described by OpenAPI, optionally sprinkled with the few links that add real value.

The deeper reason is cost versus payoff. HATEOAS optimizes for a world of long-lived, generic clients that evolve independently of the server, like the web browser following links. Most business APIs instead have a known, co-evolving set of clients where a documented contract and versioning solve coupling more directly. So teams spend their effort on OpenAPI, versioning, and good status codes, which deliver the operability they need, and skip the hypermedia machinery that would not be used.

